Pipe shells' mitred and rounded corners between pieces of a path that
are not lines or circles (mitcad#1559).

BRepFill_TrimShellCorner trims the two sides of a corner (each swept
along its piece and extended past the corner) against each other. It
ended each side's seam lines (the U-edges) at the vertex where the
corresponding seams of the two sides cross. Swept along lines or circles
(cylinders, tori) they cross exactly; swept along other curves (the
B-spline sides of an intersection curve's pieces, a sketch spline's) they
pass each other by 1e-4 to 0.2 mm, no vertex is found, the corner is left
untrimmed and the shell is invalid. A seam now ends where it crosses the
other side's faces (the new vertex the intersection put on it nearest to
the bisector plane) when the two seams have no common vertex; each side's
loop round the section then starts at its own seam's vertex, on the
section edges the two sides share.

Walking the section edges, CheckAndOrientEdges found which end of an
edge's curve on the face lies at the seam by comparing points on the face
within the resolution of the vertex's tolerance there. The curves of
approximated section edges on swept faces end up to 1.2e-6 off in the
face's parameters (4.7e-6 mm on a circle of 4 mm), beyond the resolution
of their vertices' 1e-5 mm. The ends are now compared in 3D within twice
the vertex's or the edge's tolerance, whichever is wider, and on the face
only for which of the two points they are nearer (the two curves of a
closed face's seam are one line in 3D).

An 8 mm pipe along the intersection curve of a sine wave drawn as four
lines on XZ and an arc on XY (elliptic arcs meeting at about 60 degrees
where the lines turn) was built by the caller with the section carried
unturned through the corners, leaning up to 58.8 degrees from square and
holding 23.9 % less than the circle's area times the path's length; it is
now mitred, square to the path within 0.00001 degrees, its volume within
0.00001 %. Two B-spline pieces meeting at 1 degree likewise. Where the
intersection of the two sides comes in pieces (sides of one radius touch
at two points of the corner, and meet at a shallow angle along small
corners), the loop still does not close.

